<!-- ═══ AQL EXPLAINED ═══ -->
<section style="padding: 5.5rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid #111;">
  <div class="container">
    <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 5rem; align-items: start;">
      <div>
        <p class="label">Quality Inspection</p>
        <h2 class="h2" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">AQL 2.5 — what it means in practice</h2>
        <p class="body-text" style="margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
          AQL stands for Acceptable Quality Level. It is the international standard used to determine whether a production batch passes or fails inspection. The number refers to the maximum percentage of defective units considered acceptable in a statistical sample from that batch.
        </p>
        <p class="body-text" style="margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
          AQL 2.5 — the standard SDF applies to every order — means that if more than 2.5% of the inspected sample has defects, the entire batch is rejected and reworked before packing. In practical terms, fewer than 1 in 40 garments will have a fault that leaves our facility.
        </p>
        <p class="body-text" style="margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
          Some factories use AQL 4.0, which accepts twice the defect rate. This often only becomes visible when the goods arrive and the buyer finds a substantially higher rejection rate than expected.
        </p>
        <p class="body-text">
          Our AQL inspection is done in-house by our own QC team — not by a third-party inspector who visits once and leaves. The team is present on the production floor throughout each order.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="label" style="margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">What AQL 2.5 Inspection Covers</p>
        {[
          { point: "Measurements", detail: "Every key dimension checked against approved tech pack tolerances" },
          { point: "Seam strength", detail: "Pull test on critical seams — side, shoulder, inseam, and crotch" },
          { point: "Stitching quality", detail: "Stitch density (SPI), skipped stitches, thread tension consistency" },
          { point: "Colour consistency", detail: "Shade variation between pieces and against approved bulk swatch" },
          { point: "Label placement", detail: "Woven label, care label, and size label position and legibility" },
          { point: "Fabric defects", detail: "Holes, pulls, snags, and shade differences caught pre-cut" },
          { point: "Packaging", detail: "Poly bag size, fold method, hang tag placement, carton weight" },
          { point: "Final count", detail: "Quantity verified against purchase order before container loading" },
        ].map(item => (
          <div style="padding: 0.9rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid #0f0f0f;">
